<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    FS.GG.Kit receiver (ADR-0062, .github#1262). This repo is a `coordination-kit` receiver: it holds
    the shared coordination skills and the `fsgg-coord` client on disk. It
    used to take them by byte-copy sync PR (`coordination-propagate`); it now REFERENCES the versioned
    `FS.GG.Kit` package and MATERIALIZES that kit onto disk from its pin. A hub change reaches this repo
    as an ordinary Renovate bump of the pin (in Directory.Packages.local.props), and `kit-materialize.yml`
    re-runs the materialize on that PR — the same shape lockfile-sync uses for packages.lock.json.

    This project exists ONLY to reference the package and drive its materialize. It compiles nothing and
    is deliberately NOT in the solution, so the normal build never touches it. Materialize explicitly:

        dotnet build .config/kit/FS.GG.Kit.receiver.proj -t:FsggKitMaterialize

    The materialized files (skills, scripts/fsgg-coord, and — because this is a `build-config` receiver —
    Directory.Build.props / Directory.Packages.props) are COMMITTED: the
    materialize is the write arm that replaces the byte-copy, not a live per-build input, so it does NOT
    run on every build (FsggKitMaterializeOnBuild=false, per the .github#1280 review). The committed bytes
    are content-addressed and byte-identical to canonical, so `coordination-coherence` stays green.

    `.config/dotnet-tools.json` IS NOT ON THAT LIST, AND SINCE FS.GG.Kit 0.18.0 IT IS THIS REPO'S OWN
    FILE. It used to be listed here, and that sentence outlived the fact: FS-GG/.github#1615 (ADR-0068)
    removed `dist/dotnet/.config/dotnet-tools.json` from the kit's `kit:` block, taking kit membership
    from 9 to 8, and the kit no longer writes this file into any receiver. The reason is the kit's own
    content-addressing — `registry/repos.lock` carries a digest per `kit:` source — so while the manifest
    was a kit row, moving the engine pin by ONE INTEGER was a kit content change: it staled the lock,
    reddened the hub's `main`, and bought a full republish plus a seven-receiver fan-out. Nine republishes
    over two days, four of them attributable to that row alone, and not one of the nine carried a change
    to a skill. It also made the mechanical bump structurally unmergeable — hub Renovate #1340 touched
    only the manifest and sat open for six days, because Renovate cannot run `repos.sh relock`.

    NOTHING WAS LOST WHEN THE KIT STOPPED WRITING IT. `.config/dotnet-tools.json` is a TRACKED file here
    and always has been, so the 0.18.0 bump (#1128) removed an overwrite, not a file — measured on this
    tree, where materializing 0.18.0 wrote 0 of 29 files, the required `Build-config drift check` stayed
    green, and `kit / coordination-kit` raised no drift and no "extra" finding for the now-unmanaged
    manifest. What changes is OWNERSHIP: `coordination-coherence` no longer verifies these bytes
    byte-identical to canonical, receivers' engine versions may now legitimately diverge from each
    other, and RENOVATE is what converges ours, by bumping
    `fs.gg.coord.cli` in the manifest directly. That path is not new machinery — `dotnet-tools.json` is
    one of renovate's four SHIPPED nuget `managerFilePatterns`.

    THAT LAST MILE IS NOT LIVE YET, AND SAYING SO IS THE POINT OF WRITING IT DOWN. Two do-not-propose
    rules named this file, not one: THIS repo's, in `.github/renovate.json`, narrowed by the same change
    as this comment; and the ORG-SHARED preset's, in FS-GG/.github `default.json`, scoped
    `receives=coordination-kit` and therefore covering us. The org rule was correct while the manifest
    was kit-materialized (a receiver-side bump was then a guaranteed-red PR, the `#794` churn class) and
    its premise is exactly what ADR-0068 removed; FS-GG/.github#1798 is the fix, recorded as a same-day
    correction in the ADR itself. Ours had to go REGARDLESS and first — a repo-local `packageRules` entry
    is evaluated after the preset it extends, so leaving it would keep the file disabled here no matter
    what #1798 does. Until #1798 lands, expect no engine bump PR in this repo, and read that silence as
    the known gap rather than as evidence the manifest is fine: a non-proposal is not an error and
    appears nowhere (.github#1533).

    WHAT STOPS US DELETING IT BY ACCIDENT, given that no gate here copies it any more. `.github#1077`'s
    invariant — "a `coordination-kit` receiver can actually run the engine `scripts/fsgg-coord` execs" —
    used to be ARRANGED FOR, by keeping the shim row and the manifest row on the same fabric, which could
    only ever constrain WHICH FABRIC TWO ROWS RODE and said nothing when the property broke by another
    route. ADR-0068 replaces that arrangement with an assertion: FS-GG/.github's `repos-audit`
    engine-manifest sweep reads THIS repo's real `.config/dotnet-tools.json` DAILY and reds, naming the
    repo, unless it declares `fs.gg.coord.cli` with a usable version. So deleting this file is caught
    centrally rather than silently — the one failure mode that "receiver-owned" would otherwise have
    introduced. The sweep deliberately does NOT grade the engine VERSION (that is the kit-pin freshness
    sweep's shape, one package over); a stale engine here is Renovate's job, and widening the sweep would
    red the whole fleet on the day the engine ships a version.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- A TFM is required by the SDK; nothing is compiled. Matches the repo's band (global.json). -->
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <!-- Tooling-only: no source, no output, no lockfile of its own (the gate locks the solution, not this). -->
    <EnableDefaultItems>false</EnableDefaultItems>
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>false</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>

    <!-- Materialize into the repo ROOT (two levels up from .config/kit/), on demand — never on build. -->
    <FsggKitReceiverRoot>$([System.IO.Path]::GetFullPath('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../../'))</FsggKitReceiverRoot>
    <FsggKitMaterializeOnBuild>false</FsggKitMaterializeOnBuild>

    <!-- This repo `receives: build-config`, so it also materializes Directory.Build.props /
         Directory.Packages.props to the repo root. First adoption of a hand-authored .props needs
         FsggKitAdoptBuildConfig=true for one run (.github#387); the canonical files here already carry
         the 'Source of truth: FS-GG/.github' marker, so no adopt is needed and the overwrite is free. -->
    <FsggKitMaterializeBuildConfig>true</FsggKitMaterializeBuildConfig>

    <!-- ADR-0067 phase 4 (FS-GG/.github#1676 / #1747) — THE SECOND COMMITTED SKILL ROOT IS RETIRED.
         `.agents/skills` is no longer a second committed COPY of this repo's skills: it is a VIEW
         ROOT, generated at checkout by this repo's own pinned `scripts/skill-view` from the tracked
         source `.claude/skills`. See `docs/coordination/skill-apparatus-retirement-order.md` in
         FS-GG/.github for the order, the preconditions and the rollback path.

         THE RUNTIME ROOT SET IS UNCHANGED, AND THAT IS THE POINT. `FS.GG.Kit.props` defines the
         runtime surface as the UNION `FsggKitSkillRoots` + `FsggKitViewSkillRoots`; moving a root
         from the first to the second keeps that union at ADR-0011's two as amended by ADR-0067 §5
         (`.claude/skills` + `.agents/skills`). This is NOT a root retirement — a retired root leaves
         the contract, and `.codex/skills` is the one that did (`FsggKitRetiredSkillRoots`, the kit's
         default, which swept the kit's OWN four directories out of it at f08b2a8 and left this
         repo's 46 own skills there untouched, exactly as ADR-0065 §Retiring a root requires).

         BOTH PROPERTIES ARE NAMED EXPLICITLY even though `FsggKitSkillRoots` here differs from the
         package default only by the root moved out of it. Restating the pair together is what makes
         the union readable at the one place that decides it; splitting them costs a reader a trip to
         the package's props to find out what the other half still is.

         WHY THE VIEW ROOT IS `.agents/skills` AND THE TRACKED SOURCE IS `.claude/skills`. Both roots
         hold byte-identical trees here (`diff -r .claude/skills .agents/skills` is silent, measured
         at 44981d8 before anything was retired), so either could have been the source. The retirement
         order §3 decides it for the fleet: `.claude/skills` is the source, because the kit's own
         `kit:` rows and `scripts/generate-driver-manifest` content-address that path in FS-GG/.github,
         and every receiver retired so far (Templates, Audio, Net) took the same direction. Keeping
         one direction across receivers is worth more than this repo's local preference.

         WHAT ASSERTS THE UNION IS STILL TWO. Emptying `FsggKitViewSkillRoots` below is SILENT in the
         kit's own gates — the materialize then reports "nothing to assert" and succeeds, and
         `coordination-sync` in its `check`/`against-pin` mode reads `FsggKitSkillRoots` alone and is
         green — so
         the root would simply leave the runtime contract with every required kit context still green.
         Measured on this tree, and on FS.GG.Net and FS.GG.Audio before it. That is the exact silent
         class ADR-0067 §8 forbids, so this repo asserts the union itself, with
         `scripts/skill-view check` and its `receiver-proj` flag, on the REQUIRED
         `Deterministic gate`. Change either property and that gate is what tells you. -->
    <FsggKitSkillRoots>.claude/skills</FsggKitSkillRoots>
    <FsggKitViewSkillRoots>.agents/skills</FsggKitViewSkillRoots>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Version pinned centrally in Directory.Packages.local.props (Renovate-managed), like every other pin. -->
    <PackageReference Include="FS.GG.Kit" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    THE VIEW IS GENERATED BEFORE IT IS ASSERTED, IN EVERY TREE THE MATERIALIZE RUNS IN.

    `FsggKitCheckSkillView` (ADR-0067 §8, AfterTargets=FsggKitMaterialize) fails the build when a view
    root is absent, dangling, or partly visible — deliberately, because all three of those are exit 0
    with no diagnostic in BOTH runtimes. A view root is NOT committed (a committed symlink degrades to
    a plain text file under `git -c core.symlinks=false`, ADR-0067 §6), so EVERY fresh checkout starts
    without one: a clean clone, a new worktree, and every runner alike.

    THIS REPO NEEDS IT IN THE PROJECT MORE THAN ANY RECEIVER SO FAR, because it drives the materialize
    from THREE trees and can only add a step to one of them. `.github/workflows/gate.yml`'s
    `build-config-drift` job runs `FsggKitMaterialize` on a bare checkout, and
    `.github/workflows/kit-materialize.yml` is a `uses:` of the hub's reusable workflow — a `uses:` job
    cannot be given a step at all, which is the same shape that made FS-GG/.github#1715's B5 a blocker
    rather than a tidy-up. A target here reaches all three; a workflow step reaches one.

    The tool is `scripts/skill-view` — THIS repo's copy, delivered and byte-verified by the FS.GG.Kit
    version this repo pins (a kit row since 0.15.0, together with scripts/lib/args.sh and
    scripts/lib/roots.sh, which `repos.sh validate` refuses to let a kit separate), never one fetched
    from a moving hub `main`: a receiver's verdict must be a function of the receiver
    (FS-GG/.github#1584).

    The `roots` flag names the view roots EXPLICITLY rather than letting the tool fall back to
    ADR-0011's default pair. The fallback would also try to resolve `.claude/skills`, this repo's
    TRACKED live root, and `skill-view` refuses to generate over a tracked root with no override, so it
    would be a refusal rather than a no-op.

    The `Condition` guards on the PROPERTY, so if a later change empties FsggKitViewSkillRoots this
    target stops running with it instead of generating a view nothing asserts. That same emptying is
    what `scripts/skill-view check` (with the `receiver-proj` flag) reds on, on the required
    `Deterministic gate`.
  -->
  <Target Name="FsggRenderingGenerateSkillView" BeforeTargets="FsggKitCheckSkillView"
          Condition="'$(FsggKitViewSkillRoots)' != ''">
    <Exec WorkingDirectory="$(FsggKitReceiverRoot)"
          Command="bash scripts/skill-view generate --source $(FsggKitSkillRoots) --roots &quot;$(FsggKitViewSkillRoots.Replace(';',' '))&quot;" />
  </Target>

</Project>
